 r"I am writing to apply for the Data Analyst position supporting the Aviation team at Howden. I completed an MSc in Health Data Science at University College London in September 2026, where a distinction is expected, after a BSc in Biological Sciences at Imperial College London, and I currently work as a Data Analyst Intern at TikTok Shop UK. Across my academic and professional experience I have turned messy datasets into reports that people act on, and used AI tools to take the repetitive work out of the process.",

 r"What attracts me most to this role is that the analysis has a visible end point: accurate, timely reporting that brokers and senior management use for renewals, lineslips and market presentations. The posting asks for someone who can break problems into steps an agent can execute and use good judgement about when to lean on AI, and that is exactly how I have been working. I enjoy taking an unclear business problem, identifying what users actually need, and turning it into a workflow that can be tested and improved.",

 r"At TikTok Shop UK I analyse large merchant and transaction datasets in SQL and Python and build the KPI frameworks and dashboards that operations, product and commercial teams use to set targets and track progress. When my supervisor asked me to configure a campaign for merchants by hand, I worked through a few examples, saw the underlying logic and used Claude Code to build a multi-step agent with a validation step to check its own output, which cut the time the task took by around 40 to 50 percent. Showing a sceptical colleague that the output was accurate mattered as much as building it, and it taught me to keep a human review step in any automated workflow.",

 r"At DS Digital Technology I worked as a Data Scientist Intern for a pharmaceutical client. I built a regex and LLM extraction pipeline that turned more than 4,800 free-text patient consultation threads into structured data, scored more than 2,400 AI responses against a benchmark and rubric the client adopted as its acceptance standard, and presented the findings to clinical and business stakeholders. Working in an unfamiliar specialist domain alongside experts taught me to learn the context quickly by asking the people who know it best, which is how I would approach aviation insurance with Emma and the wider team.",

 r"My MSc dissertation used 458,840 UK Biobank participants, which meant a great deal of time on data quality: reconciling variables across assessment visits, handling missing data and checking that the cohort I was analysing was the one I thought it was. At Ipsos Strategy3 I structured data from more than 80 healthcare professional interviews, built market-sizing models and turned the analysis into client reports. Both taught me to check the data before trusting any result built on it, and to present findings in the language of the people who will use them.",

